#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33ffd934-5f64-58c1-bfa8-cf43ec6a7b06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275706} {:answer/id #uuid "b29bb4de-e2e0-56ba-9282-b1fdfe9636df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275707} {:answer/id #uuid "0768e085-457d-5566-8f90-19a74d61576f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275708} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e3162a-4e7a-5564-9884-12464ab6d7b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275709} {:answer/id #uuid "90c1c08b-70e3-5e52-9c96-014e226f89a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b29bb4de-e2e0-56ba-9282-b1fdfe9636df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275707} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e5faff8-f4dc-5206-83e4-4a1fbb981af5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c16c119-1378-5409-b295-b09c8e50af75" :question/math-academy-id "q-115530" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt{x}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{ln}^{2}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{{[{ln}^{2}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{4\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{[4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}{(\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{8\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt{x} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02299284-7905-5e23-a99b-fab257fad339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275713} {:answer/id #uuid "45f7f7ff-fa85-556d-ac9b-fb6cf116a017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275714} {:answer/id #uuid "96b91ed0-23a5-5a64-b958-be50242ffb32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275715} {:answer/id #uuid "19d23dce-89ec-5bf3-8b43-cb25fdebb900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275716} {:answer/id #uuid "29f955b3-1f9d-5ebc-9826-4d116af6cf84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19d23dce-89ec-5bf3-8b43-cb25fdebb900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275716} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3289c5e-cad9-5a38-9f0d-b79099f92a16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1e8835d-2849-5c15-b2b3-5060e0821698" :question/math-academy-id "q-31585" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}-x-1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}-x-1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[{e}^{x}-x-1]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}-1}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}-x-1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1} & =-{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[{e}^{x}-1]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{0}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5df862c-e599-5956-bcd4-c6e6ea300a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5df862c-e599-5956-bcd4-c6e6ea300a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275720} :answer-field/id #uuid "6692dc66-2864-55c0-918e-b77305481098" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f06dd217-55b8-5f77-84e6-dcab94f6c615" :question/math-academy-id "q-336935" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)]}^{′}}{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)]}^{′}}{{[(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89e58617-4349-519b-803a-00f0feaf28dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275723} {:answer/id #uuid "57d362d0-f1da-5400-9004-ee0218c88d87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275724} {:answer/id #uuid "9228ff48-7005-5a36-be2b-4de15ca746f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275725} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4d198e-b15b-5662-9110-b93ba3a9eba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275726} {:answer/id #uuid "79444b30-53a2-5fa7-871a-c6436654074e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57d362d0-f1da-5400-9004-ee0218c88d87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275724} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb670e21-a172-5934-91ac-63ce37493d5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e565d8e2-3b12-5048-98de-b990b804380c" :question/math-academy-id "q-3778" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{x+16}=$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{x+16} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{2}-1]^{′}}{[x+16]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd470956-f46a-524f-93f6-7cfea4f61dc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275730} {:answer/id #uuid "cad86e4c-2b0f-506d-8803-4288769136a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275731} {:answer/id #uuid "653ab907-40cd-5543-b0cd-e07538abe574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275732} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0555b1-3de4-5f74-b04d-e9711990e5ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275733} {:answer/id #uuid "32d005f8-8c9d-5b1c-a2d5-30ae5e4fe9cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd470956-f46a-524f-93f6-7cfea4f61dc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275730} :answer-field/id #uuid "76b9f4c3-b07d-5a7b-a386-ed4be8f82892" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5460231d-5ab3-5dda-afe5-f1c42598ed15" :question/math-academy-id "q-72335" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}}{(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})}=$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to 0}{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}}{(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}})}{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}\\cancel{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}}{\\cancel{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}} \\\\  & =4{lim}_{x\\to 0}{e}^{2/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
